"""Slides (1920x1080 html) and narration text for the final video of topic 2. usage: cd video/final && python make_slides.py ; then render PNGs (see render.sh), tts_kokoro.py, build.sh."""
import os
BG, FG, MUT, ACC, ACC2, LINE = '#1B2430', '#F6F4EE', '#C9D1DB', '#F2A65A', '#7FB7BE', '#3A4656'
CSS = f"""html,body{{margin:0;background:#000}}section{{box-sizing:border-box;width:1920px;height:1080px;overflow:hidden;background:{BG};color:{FG};
font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;padding:90px 120px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:34px}}
h1{{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:64px;margin:0;font-weight:600;line-height:1.12}}h2{{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:58px;margin:0;font-weight:600;line-height:1.12}}
p,li{{font-size:34px;line-height:1.38;margin:0;color:{MUT}}}ul{{margin:0;padding-left:44px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px}}
.k{{font-size:24px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;color:{ACC}}}b{{color:{FG}}}.s{{font-size:26px;color:#98A4B3}}
.row{{display:flex;gap:70px}}.col{{flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:22px}}
.bar{{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:20px;font-size:30px;color:{MUT}}}.bar .l{{width:300px}}.bar .t{{height:34px;border-radius:0 6px 6px 0}}.bar .v{{color:{FG}}}
table{{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:30px;color:{MUT}}}th,td{{padding:12px 26px;border-bottom:1px solid {LINE};text-align:left}}th{{color:{FG}}}"""
def bars(rows, scale=520, color=ACC, mx=None):
    mx = mx or max(v for _, v in rows)
    return ''.join(f'<div class=bar><span class=l>{l}</span><span class=t style="width:{int(scale*v/mx)}px;background:{color}"></span><span class=v>{v:.3f}</span></div>' for l, v in rows)
def slide(n, body, k='', text=''):
    open(f'frames/{n:02d}.html', 'w').write(f'<!doctype html><meta charset=utf-8><style>{CSS}</style><section>' + (f'<div class=k>{k}</div>' if k else '') + body + '</section>')
    open(f'script/{n:02d}.txt', 'w').write(text.strip() + '\n')
slide(1, f"""<div style="flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;gap:34px"><div class=k>Final report · music generation</div>
<h1 style="font-size:92px">Which stem carries the era of a song, and can we regenerate it?</h1>
<p>Stem-wise analysis of release decade and release market, and era-targeted covers: keep the vocal and melody, regenerate the accompaniment.</p></div>
<div class=s>James Christian · Liang-Yu Hsia · open to collaborators</div>""",
text="Hello. This is our final report on a question about recordings and their release era. Which part of a song, the vocal or the accompaniment, tells a model its release decade or release market? And if we keep the original vocal and melody, can a generator rewrite the accompaniment in a chosen decade? We answer with probes on frozen audio encoders, interventions on the stems, and a cover generation system with several independent checks.")
slide(2, f"""<h2>Data and what the labels mean</h2><div class=row><div class=col><ul>
<li><b>Dataset A:</b> six release decades, 1960s to 2010s.</li><li><b>Dataset B:</b> six release markets, all from the 1980s: US, UK, Brazil, Spain, Germany, Italy.</li>
<li>Thirty second clips from Discogs-VI recordings, artist-disjoint splits; test labels are hidden, so all accuracies are on validation: 132 and 102 clips.</li></ul></div>
<div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">In the full Discogs-VI metadata</p><ul><li><b>2.42</b> release countries per composition on average</li><li><b>1.99</b> release decades per composition</li>
<li>So a label describes an <b>edition</b>, not a composition.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Setup',
"We use thirty second clips from Discogs VI recordings. Dataset A has six release decades, from the nineteen sixties to the twenty tens. Dataset B has six release markets, all from the nineteen eighties: United States, United Kingdom, Brazil, Spain, Germany and Italy. Test labels are hidden, so every accuracy is on the validation split, with a hundred thirty two and a hundred two clips. One caution about labels: in the full Discogs VI metadata, a composition has on average two point four two release countries and almost two release decades. So these labels describe editions, not compositions.")
slide(3, f"""<h2>Decade lives in the accompaniment, market in the vocal</h2><div class=row><div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Decade (A), MERT, validation accuracy (chance .167)</p>{bars([('mix',.477),('vocals',.394),('accompaniment',.485)],mx=.7)}</div>
<div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Market (B), MERT</p>{bars([('mix',.588),('vocals',.608),('accompaniment',.422)],mx=.7,color='#7FB7BE')}</div></div>
<p>MuQ shows the same ordering with smaller effects. A second separator reproduces it, but vocal probes lose accuracy when the separator changes.</p>""", 'Phase 1 · stem probes',
"We separate each clip with H T Demucs, embed the stems with MERT and MuQ, and fit linear probes. For decade, the accompaniment reaches point four eight five with MERT, as good as the mix at point four seven seven, while the vocal is lower at point three nine four. For market, the vocal reaches point six oh eight, as good as the mix, and the accompaniment is lower at point four two two. After Holm correction, the market contrasts with MERT are significant. A second separator gives the same ordering, but vocal probes lose accuracy when the separator changes, so absolute stem numbers depend on the separator.")
slide(4, f"""<h2>What the probes actually use</h2><div class=row><div class=col><ul>
<li><b>Stem swap.</b> Decade follows the accompaniment: 34.8% vs 16.4% for the vocal. Market follows the vocal: 50.0% vs 18.3%.</li>
<li><b>Erasing sung language</b> (rank 5): market accuracy of the mix falls from .588 to .353; random directions of the same rank: .591.</li></ul></div>
<div class=col><ul><li><b>English-sung clips only:</b> market accuracy .397, majority share .274.</li><li><b>Decade</b> also travels with genre and with recording bandwidth: band-limiting and hiss move the probes to earlier decades.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Phase 1 · interventions',
"To see which cue is used, we swapped stems. Take the vocal of one clip and the accompaniment of another clip with a different label. For decade, the prediction follows the accompaniment: thirty five percent against sixteen percent for the vocal. For market it follows the vocal: fifty percent against eighteen. Erasing the linear subspace of sung language cuts market accuracy for the mix from point five eight eight to point three five three, while erasing random directions of the same size changes nothing. For English sung clips only, market accuracy falls to point three nine seven. So market is largely sung language. Decade also travels with genre and with recording bandwidth.")
slide(5, f"""<h2>The cover system</h2><div class=row><div class=col><ul><li>Keep the <b>original vocal</b>; regenerate the accompaniment with <b>MusicGen-Melody</b>.</li>
<li>Input: <b>chroma of the original mix</b> and a text prompt for the era.</li><li>A <b>low-rank adapter</b> trained on training accompaniment, prompt = the clip's decade.</li></ul></div>
<div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Three-branch guidance</p><p style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:38px;color:{FG}">logits = null + g<sub>m</sub> (neutral − null) + g<sub>e</sub> (era − neutral)</p>
<p>The melody term g<sub>m</sub> = 3 stays, so the song stays recognisable; the era term g<sub>e</sub> pushes toward the decade. Contrast-only guidance lowers song identification from .875 to about .67.</p></div></div>""", 'Phase 2 · generation',
"For generation we keep the original vocal. MusicGen Melody gets the chroma of the original mix and a text prompt for the era, and regenerates the accompaniment. We add two things. A low rank adapter, trained on the accompaniment of the training clips with their decade as the prompt. And a three branch guidance rule. The standard melody guidance term stays, so the song stays recognisable, and an era contrast term is added on top with its own weight. Contrast only guidance, without the melody term, lowers song identification top one from point eight seven five to about point six seven. So the melody term matters.")
slide(6, f"""<h2>Change in target-decade probability vs a neutral prompt</h2><div class=row><div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">MERT probe, 24 songs × 3 seeds</p><table><tr><th></th><th>1960s</th><th>1980s</th><th>2010s</th></tr>
<tr><td>base model</td><td>−.001</td><td>+.593</td><td>+.328</td></tr><tr><td>adapter</td><td>+.392</td><td>+.253</td><td>+.120</td></tr><tr><td>+ guidance, g<sub>e</sub> 7</td><td>+.618</td><td>+.517</td><td>+.353</td></tr><tr><td>+ guidance, g<sub>e</sub> 10</td><td>+.698</td><td>+.648</td><td>+.424</td></tr></table></div>
<div class=col><ul><li>The base model follows the 1980s, partly the 2010s, <b>not the 1960s</b>.</li><li>MuQ gives the same pattern; the guidance gain replicates on two more adapter seeds.</li><li>Probes are codec-matched: trained on real accompaniment after the same codec round trip.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Results',
"We score each clip with decade probes trained on real accompaniment passed through the same codec, using two encoders, and report the change in the probability of the target decade against a neutral prompt. The unmodified model follows the nineteen eighties prompt strongly, plus point five nine three with MERT, the twenty tens moderately, and the nineteen sixties not at all. The adapter makes the sixties work. Adding three branch guidance raises every decade. At era weight ten, the gains are plus point six nine eight for the sixties, plus point six four eight for the eighties, and plus point four two four for the twenty tens. The gain replicates on two more adapter training seeds.")
slide(7, f"""<h2>Six prompted decades</h2><div style="display:flex;justify-content:center"><img src="six_decades_E.png" style="height:640px"></div>
<p>With the adapter, the expected probe decade rises monotonically with the prompted decade; the base model does not. The 1990s prompt is the weakest.</p>""", 'Results',
"Extending to six prompts, with the adapter the expected decade of the probe increases monotonically from the sixties prompt to the twenty tens prompt. This holds in both encoders, and in an independent third encoder. The unmodified model does not. Its nineteen seventies prompt even gives a later decade than the neutral prompt. The nineteen nineties prompt is the weakest. Thin dashed lines show two other adapter training seeds, which behave the same.")
slide(8, f"""<h2>Does the song stay the song?</h2><div class=row><div class=col><ul><li>Song identification from the cover alone: top-1 <b>.875</b> (neutral .944, chance .042) at g<sub>e</sub> 7 and 10.</li>
<li>Audiobox production quality does not drop (7.50 to 7.52 vs 7.31 neutral).</li></ul></div><div class=col><ul><li>FD-VGG against real clips of the decade falls for the <b>1980s and 2010s</b> prompts, not for the 1960s.</li>
<li>Clips stay close to their original (KL of PaSST classes lower than neutral).</li><li>We recommend g<sub>e</sub> 7 to 10; at 20 identity and the FD gains weaken.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Results',
"The song stays the song. Song identification from the cover alone is point eight seven five, against point nine four four for neutral generations of the same adapter; chance is point oh four two. Audiobox production quality does not drop. Against real clips of the prompted decade, the Fréchet distance falls for the nineteen eighties and twenty tens prompts, but not for the sixties. The covers also stay close to their originals under a classifier distance. We recommend era weight seven to ten. At twenty, identity and the distribution gains weaken.")
slide(9, f"""<h2>What limits the claim</h2><ul><li><b>Bandwidth:</b> prompts with no decade, only “dull, band-limited, tape hiss”, shift the probes by about half the 1960s prompt effect (MERT −1.34 vs −2.79 expected decades).</li>
<li><b>Spectrum matching</b> keeps the MERT 1980s and 2010s effects positive; the MuQ 2010s effect halves.</li><li><b>Wording:</b> for the 1990s, a grunge wording gives the weakest effect and a eurodance wording the strongest in MERT; no wording wins in both encoders.</li>
<li><b>Other generators:</b> MuseControlLite keeps the melody but barely moves the probes; ACE-Step moves them but keeps much less of the song.</li></ul>""", 'Controls',
"Several controls limit the claims. First, prompts that name no decade, only bandwidth, such as dull, band limited, with tape hiss, shift the probes by about half the effect of the sixties prompt. So part of the era effect rides on bandwidth. Second, matching the long term spectrum of each clip to its neutral clip keeps the MERT eighties and twenty tens effects positive, but halves the MuQ twenty tens effect. Third, wording matters. For the nineties, a grunge wording gives the weakest effect and a eurodance wording the strongest in MERT, and no wording wins in both encoders. Two other generators behave differently: MuseControlLite keeps the melody but barely moves the probes, and ACE Step moves them but keeps much less of the song.")
slide(10, f"""<h2>Limits</h2><ul><li>24 validation songs, 3 seeds; one validation split per task; no artist ids; test labels hidden.</li><li>Probes are proxies for era, trained on the same real clips whose labels they predict; language and genre are proxies too.</li>
<li>One generator family carries the main result.</li><li><b>No listening test has been run:</b> we make no claim about how the covers sound to people.</li></ul>""", 'Limits',
"The limits are clear. Twenty four validation songs and three seeds. One validation split per task. No artist ids, and hidden test labels. The probes are proxies for era, trained on the same real clips whose labels they predict, and language and genre are proxies as well. One generator family carries the main result. And no listening test has been run, so we make no claim about how the covers sound to people.")
slide(11, f"""<div style="flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;gap:30px"><h1>Summary</h1><ul><li>Decade is read mostly from the <b>accompaniment</b>; market mostly from the <b>vocal</b>, through sung language.</li>
<li>An adapter with melody-preserving era guidance steers a cover along the decade axis with the song mostly kept.</li><li>Part of the effect rides on bandwidth; no listening test yet.</li></ul>
<p class=s>Report, code, demo clips: goog-msft-fb-nflx-nvda-aapl.github.io/musicgen-steering-eval/era_market · open to collaborators</p></div>""", 'Summary',
"In short. Decade is read mostly from the accompaniment, and market mostly from the vocal, through sung language. An adapter with melody preserving era guidance can steer a cover along the decade axis with the song mostly kept. Part of the effect rides on bandwidth, and there is no listening test yet. The report, code and demo clips are on our project page. We are open to collaborators. Thank you.")
